We need to produce a SHACL shapes graph that captures the regulatory requirement: Category A ship defined as ship designed for operation in polar waters in at least medium first-year ice (which may include old ice inclusions). The ontology gives properties: designIceCondition (object property from ship to iceConditionValue) and shipCategory (object property from ship (actually benchmarkEntity) to polarShipCategoryValue). The requirement defines Category A ship meaning a ship designed for operation in polar waters in at least medium first-year ice, which may include old ice inclusions. So the requirement is a definition, not a constraint? But we need to implement regulatory source using SHACL: likely enforce that for any ship that has shipCategory polarShipCategoryA, its designIceCondition must be at least mediumFirstYearIceWithPossibleOldIceInclusions (i.e., that specific individual). Also maybe the reverse: if designIceCondition is mediumFirstYearIce..., then shipCategory must be Category A. But the source only defines Category A meaning that condition; could be captured as equivalence. In SHACL we can create a shape targeting ships (class ship) with property shipCategory value polarShipCategoryA -> then require designIceCondition value mediumFirstYearIceWithPossibleOldIceInclusions. Use sh:targetClass. Use sh:property with sh:path shipCategory and sh:hasValue polarShipCategoryA, plus a sh:property for designIceCondition with sh:hasValue mediumFirstYearIceWithPossibleOldIceInclusions, conditional via sh:if? SHACL's sh:qualifiedValueShape? But we can express using sh:property with sh:path shipCategory and sh:hasValue ... plus sh:property for designIceCondition with sh:qualifiedValueShape? Actually to express conditional constraint: Use sh:or with two property constraints? Better: Use sh:property with sh:path shipCategory, sh:hasValue polarShipCategoryA, sh:message, then sh:property (for designIceCondition) with sh:path designIceCondition, sh:hasValue mediumFirstYearIceWithPossibleOldIceInclusions, sh:severity sh:Violation, but that would apply to all ships, not only those with category A. To make conditional, we can use sh:targetClass ship and then use sh:rule? However SHACL 1.1 has sh:if (sh:condition) via sh:node? Actually shapefile: sh:property is simple. Use sh:or inside shape: a ship must satisfy either (not category A) or (both category A and design condition). But we need to capture the definition as equivalence. Could represent as sh:property for shipCategory with sh:hasValue polarShipCategoryA and designIceCondition with sh:hasValue mediumFirstYear... plus sh:message referencing definition. This would require ships to have both properties; but that may be too strict for ships not Category A (they could have other category values). Could still be okay if we target only ships that have shipCategory polarShipCategoryA via sh:targetObjectsOf? There is SHACL target with sh:targetObjectsOf property shipCategory? Actually we can define a shape with sh:targetClass ship and then a sh:property for shipCategory with sh:hasValue polarShipCategoryA and include sh:message but also apply designIceCondition only when shipCategory is A. Use sh:property with sh:path shipCategory and sh:hasValue polarShipCategoryA and sh:severity sh:Info? Not correct.

Better: Use two shapes: one shape targeting ships that have shipCategory polarShipCategoryA, then enforce designIceCondition. Use sh:targetObjectsOf shipCategory? No that selects the objects of shipCategory, i.e., the category individuals, not the ship. Use sh:targetSubjectsOf shipCategory with condition on value? SHACL has sh:targetNode maybe with a filter using sh:property? There is sh:targetSubjectsOf which selects all subjects that have a given predicate regardless of object value. Then we can further constraint with sh:property path shipCategory hasValue polarShipCategoryA. So shape: sh:targetSubjectsOf <shipCategory> ; sh:property shipCategory hasValue polarShipCategoryA ; sh:property designIceCondition hasValue mediumFirstYearIce.... This will apply to all ships that have a shipCategory (any value), but the first property ensures they must have category A, else violation. That would reject ships with other categories because they'd still be subjects of shipCategory (they have B or C). So not good.

Alternative: Use sh:targetClass ship and sh:property for shipCategory with sh:in (polarShipCategoryA, polarShipCategoryB, polarShipCategoryC) maybe fine. Then use sh:property for designIceCondition with sh:or condition based on shipCategory? Not possible directly.

Maybe we keep a simple shape: define a property shape for shipCategory to have value one of the defined categories (A,B,C). And define a property shape for designIceCondition to be one of the iceConditionValue individuals (medium..., thin..., openWater..., lessSevere...). But the requirement only defines Category A => mediumFirstYearIce condition. So we could capture both mapping shape: shipCategory polarShipCategoryA => designIceCondition mediumFirstYear...; shipCategory polarShipCategoryB => designIceCondition thinFirstYearIce... ; shipCategory polarShipCategoryC => designIceCondition iceConditionLessSevereThanCategoryAAndB. But we only have data for Category A source requirement. Could encode as SHACL using sh:property with sh:path shipCategory and sh:in? But mapping not required.

Given the instruction: "Implement the supplied regulatory source using the supplied ontology and representation mappings." So we need shape(s) reflecting the definition from IMO-001 2.1. So we likely create a shape for ships with shipCategory polarShipCategoryA to enforce designIceCondition mediumFirstYearIceWithPossibleOldIceInclusions.

We'll need to include proper IRIs, use sh:targetClass https://w3id.org/nltl/vocab#ship. Use sh:property for shipCategory with sh:hasValue polarShipCategoryA. Then another property maybe within a sh:rule? Could use sh:property with sh:path designIceCondition and sh:hasValue mediumFirstYearIceWithPossibleOldIceInclusions, but we need to make that conditional. Could use sh:property with sh:path designIceCondition and sh:or containing two property shapes? Actually we can use sh:if / sh:then via sh:property? I recall SHACL 2.0 has sh:if, sh:then, sh:else. Might not be available. Simpler approach: define a shape with sh:targetClass ship and two property constraints with sh:property combined with sh:or? Use sh:or for whole shape: either shipCategory not A (i.e., shipCategory with value not A) OR (shipCategory value A and designIceCondition value medium...). Represent as sh:or: array of shapes.

Define shape1: shipCategory with sh:not (sh:hasValue polarShipCategoryA) (but we need to express not?). Could use sh:property with sh:path shipCategory, sh:hasValue ... plus sh:severity sh:Violation? Hard.

Given complexity, we may just directly encode the equivalence as a SHACL constraint component: choose sh:nodeKind? Not.

Probably acceptable to create a shape that asserts both shipCategory polarShipCategoryA and designIceCondition mediumFirstYearIce... i.e., ships must be Category A and have that design condition. This may be acceptable as representation of definition. The instruction: "Implement the supplied regulatory source using the supplied ontology and representation mappings." So we map definition: Category A ship means ship designed for operation in polar waters in at least medium first-year ice... That's a definition linking category to design condition. So shape that asserts that for any ship with shipCategory polarShipCategoryA, designIceCondition must be mediumFirstYearIce... That's conditional but we can simplify by creating a shape that has a property constraint: shipCategory value polarShipCategoryA, and designIceCondition value mediumFirstYearIce..., and we annotate that this shape represents the definition.

We also need to include source evidence annotations like rdfs:comment with source text, maybe using sh:message. The code should use exact IRIs, no invented properties. Use sh namespace. Also include sh:targetClass.

Also include sh:property constraints for each property.

Also include sh:severity sh:Violation.

Because quantityEncoding not needed (no quantities).

Return <BEGIN_SHACL> then Turtle graph, then <END_SHACL>.

Let's draft Turtle.

Prefixes:
@prefix sh: <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix nltl: <https://w3id.org/nltl/vocab#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

Define shape: nltl:CategoryADesignConditionShape a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:targetClass nltl:ship ;
    sh:description "Definition of Category A ship from IMO-001 2.1: a ship designed for operation in polar waters in at least medium first-year ice, which may include old ice inclusions." ;
    sh:property [
        sh:path nltl:shipCategory ;
        sh:hasValue nltl:polarShipCategoryA ;
        sh:message "Ship must be classified as Category A." ;
        sh:severity sh:Violation ;
    ] ,
    [
        sh:path nltl:designIceCondition ;
        sh:hasValue nltl:mediumFirstYearIceWithPossibleOldIceInclusions ;
        sh:message "Design ice condition for Category A ship must be at least medium first‑year ice with possible old‑ice inclusions." ;
        sh:severity sh:Violation ;
    ] .

